Three patterns emerge. First, $\eta_{\max}$ remains significant in $\geq$10 of 12 conditions under every aggregation method---the weakest-link channel is robust to the choice of decision rule. Second, Bayesian calibration sharply suppresses the diversity channel: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance drops from 12/12 (majority) to 5/12 (Bayesian), and the coefficient direction flips to negative in 3 conditions, meaning diversity becomes partially protective when judge reliability is properly calibrated. Third, $R^2$ drops from 0.433 (majority) to 0.141 (Bayesian), indicating that Bayesian aggregation homogenizes the ASR distribution across panels---compositional factors explain less variance, though the residual variance is harder to predict.

These results have a direct prescriptive implication. Since Bayesian calibration suppresses the diversity channel without weakening the weakest-link channel, a two-pronged defense is needed: robustness-first panel selection to address $\eta_{\max}$, combined with Bayesian aggregation to mitigate the $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ vulnerability. Neither intervention alone addresses both drivers.

